WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.098
- [Kelly] Have a seat.

00:00:01.098 --> 00:00:02.496
- [Seal] Thank you.

00:00:02.496 --> 00:00:04.590
- Oh my God, I love your outfit.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:05.423
- Thank you.

00:00:05.423 --> 00:00:06.660
- Those are the pants
I love 'cause you can

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.000
just go (mimics explosion)
and they're off.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:09.833
- You can?

00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:12.420
- Yeah. Those are the button ones, right?

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.070
You know, like the
button ones on the sides

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:14.903
like when you play sports.

00:00:14.903 --> 00:00:17.164
Anyway, I love those. It's
nice to see you again.

00:00:17.164 --> 00:00:18.090
- Nice to see you again, yeah.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.410
- We haven't seen each other,

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:20.970
I think we met and we were doing something

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.158
with David Foster for-

00:00:22.158 --> 00:00:24.480
- That was it. Something he roped us into.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.770
- And we showed up, but we,

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.910
it was a race situation that
we both knew a lot about.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.679
- Can you remember the
song we were singing?

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:33.600
What were we singing?

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.433
- I don't.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.660
It wasn't like "The Star Spangled Banner"

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:37.607
or anything, was it?

00:00:37.607 --> 00:00:38.787
- No.
- No?

00:00:38.787 --> 00:00:39.887
It wasn't something...

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.780
'Cause they do that at
some of those events.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.070
- Yeah. It was good fun though.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.870
- Totally, it was
memorable for both of us.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.243
We can't remember what we sang.

00:00:47.243 --> 00:00:48.390
(Kelly laughs)
(audience laughs)

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.160
It was so long ago.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.010
I mean like, probably 2004 or 5.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.093
- Yeah, probably.

00:00:55.272 --> 00:00:57.030
At least 16, 17 years ago.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.410
- Yeah, it was a while back.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:00.120
Anyway, your voice is incredible.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.650
I was so honored to get to sing with you.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.200
I love, there's people that can sing,

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.033
like, I feel like I'm a singer,

00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:06.780
but you have, you're like a stylist.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.580
You have that tone that
everybody recognizes

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.413
right off the bat, you know?

00:01:09.413 --> 00:01:10.351
It's so special.

00:01:10.351 --> 00:01:11.280
- You're not so bad yourself.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.650
- Well, your tone though
is just incredible.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.260
Do you remember the first time
you ever performed anywhere?

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:17.093
Like when you were a kid?

00:01:17.093 --> 00:01:19.260
- Yeah. I remember the
first time singing live.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.230
I had this teacher at
school called Mr. Ren.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:26.550
And I idolized him and
I wanted to be like him.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.950
He was the kind of teacher
that all the guy teachers

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.950
wanted to be and all the girl
teachers wanted to be with,

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.300
that kind of guy, you know?

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.120
If we're lucky in our lives,

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.720
we all have that one person
that sees us, you know?

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.210
And he saw me and he sang.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.684
And I wanted to be just like him.

00:01:44.684 --> 00:01:45.990
- That's so cool.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.360
- So I started singing, then
he would encourage me to sing

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:53.360
and he'd put me on stage to
sing a song by Johnny Nash

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:56.010
at the Parents and Teachers
Association Evening.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.250
Now, my parents had never
heard me sing before.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:02.220
They didn't even know that I sang.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.070
And I remember him calling
my name to the stage.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.500
He said, "And now we're
gonna have young Seal

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.009
and he is gonna sing
this Johnny Nash song."

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:11.021
"I Can See Clearly." You know that?

00:02:11.021 --> 00:02:16.021
♪ I can see clearly now the rain is gone ♪

00:02:16.512 --> 00:02:21.420
♪ It's gonna be bright
bright sunshiny day ♪

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.130
- Such a great song.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:23.963
- So he goes-

00:02:23.963 --> 00:02:26.160
- Feel free to do that at any
moment during this interview.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.160
It sounds so sexy. It's so cool.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.469
It is. Your rasp.

00:02:30.469 --> 00:02:32.070
(audience cheers)

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.090
You know what it is?

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.920
When I'm tired, like I'm tired today,

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.960
but when I'm tired I love
singing when I'm tired

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.060
'cause I feel like I
can sound cool like you

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.590
or like, maybe Toni Braxton.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:43.620
You know that low raspy, just like,

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:44.453
I don't have that most of the time.

00:02:44.453 --> 00:02:46.050
Keep going. So sorry.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.640
How did you do with the
song when you were a kid?

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:48.690
Were you nervous on the stage?

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:49.770
- Yeah, I was.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.390
I remember him calling me,

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.700
and it's like that scene in a movie where,

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.080
you know, everything goes silent

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.317
and you can hear a pin drop, you know,

00:02:57.317 --> 00:02:59.520
'cause it was acapella and no one

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.200
had ever heard me sing
before, so I remember-

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.820
- You didn't even sing with
music for the first time?

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:03.903
- No, no.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:05.790
- [Kelly] Bold.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.580
- So he called me on stage
and I remember standing there

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.180
and looking out and seeing
everyone in the audience

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.350
pretty much like I'm seeing you except

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:14.553
there were more of you.

00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:20.519
And I just, I wanted
the stage to just open

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.533
and swallow me up, you know?

00:03:23.435 --> 00:03:25.740
It was the last place I wanted
to be and I was kind of like

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.820
I was frozen, but somehow
I closed my eyes and got

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:34.380
through it and sang
and kept my eyes closed

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.580
at the end of it.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.470
And then just everyone applauded

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:38.880
and I remember looking out and seeing

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.567
my parents going (claps).

00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:41.913
- (laughs) Like, what?

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.740
- And the interesting thing
about that experience is,

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.210
is that that place,

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:52.890
that stage that felt
like the most terrifying,

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:56.070
most lonely place all of
a sudden felt like home.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.990
And I never forgot that feeling.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:01.590
So I guess throughout my
life, I did different things

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.350
until I eventually decided I
was gonna commit to singing.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.570
But I never forgot that feeling.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.450
And I always knew that I
would come back to that.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:10.470
- I love that story.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.020
But I also love that you
pointed out it is like

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.180
if one person sees you and you're younger

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:18.060
and it gives you confidence
that you can do something

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.280
and maybe you're special
and every kid likes to

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:21.187
feel special, right?

00:04:21.187 --> 00:04:22.020
- Of course.

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:24.000
- It does take one
person, I think to like,

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.260
I mean it's good if it's more than one,

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.450
but if it's just one
that's nice that, you know,

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.700
you feel like you maybe found something

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.350
that you're special at.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:32.633
That's what happened to me too.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:34.830
- And you're absolutely right-

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.240
- It changes your personality, everything.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.270
- Yeah, it really comes
down to who, you know,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.220
that champion or those champions

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:44.890
in your life that do see
you and encourage you

00:04:47.222 --> 00:04:48.360
to be special.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.910
And who knows, if not for Mr. Ren,

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:54.060
perhaps, you know, it
wouldn't have happened for me.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.136
I certainly wouldn't
have had that experience.

00:04:56.136 --> 00:05:00.360
And throughout my life I've
had timely people who have

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.890
come and championed me and
have seen me in different ways

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.980
and given me the inspiration
and the courage to move on

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:11.490
which is why I feel so
strongly about mentoring.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:12.323
- Yeah.

00:05:15.244 --> 00:05:18.180
- I found out fairly recently that as much

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.630
as I love singing and as
much as I love performing

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:26.630
and as much as music is a part of my life,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.013
I really love coaching.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:35.430
I would much rather see somebody
else transcend whatever's

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:38.820
holding them back than doing it myself.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.400
I get more pleasure out of that.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.220
- That's why I love being
a part of "The Voice."

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:45.360
'Cause it's like every,

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.580
and especially 'cause I
came from that environment,

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.340
like it's the most awesome
thing to be blessed

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:51.173
in your career.

00:05:51.173 --> 00:05:53.610
A lot of people can sing, a lot
of people can do what we do.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:54.840
Don't have your tone, but like, you know,

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.130
a lot of people can do what we do,

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:58.710
and it's cool to be able to look

00:05:58.710 --> 00:05:59.827
at someone younger and go,

00:05:59.827 --> 00:06:01.710
"Let me show you what
maybe you're not seeing,

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:02.543
that little light in you."

00:06:02.543 --> 00:06:04.230
And it's so cool to be able
to bring that out of people.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.210
It is so gratifying.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.340
All right, so your biggest
song "Kiss from a Rose"

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:09.510
was from your sophomore album,

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.880
which usually is kind of a
tough album for everybody,

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.400
but this is like your biggest
song on your sophomore album.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.423
That's incredible.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:19.560
- Thank you.
- It's an incredible thing.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:22.110
- I got really lucky.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.550
I got really, really lucky

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.940
because as you say, you know,
we call it the sophomore jinx,

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:28.680
the second album generally tanks

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.830
especially if you've had a big first album

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.470
which in my case I did.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:36.570
Anyway, came to recording the second,

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.370
or releasing the second album.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.620
We released four singles from it,

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.067
I think it was "Prayer for the Dying,"

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:44.160
"Newborn Friend," "Show Me"

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.910
and this weird little song
called "Kiss from a Rose."

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:50.940
And I remember the song
"Kiss from a Rose,"

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:55.200
it charted at number
60, dropped to number 80

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.270
the next week and was done.

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:58.260
It was out of the charts.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:01.301
I did this terrible video
for it, which didn't help.

00:07:01.301 --> 00:07:02.134
(audience laughs)

00:07:02.134 --> 00:07:03.870
And the song was done.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:07.440
I was in the studio
recording the third album.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.360
Now the first album had sold I think

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.940
about 7 million or thereabouts, you know,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.430
which in those days it was pretty big.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.770
- Now it's unheard of.
People don't buy albums.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:19.833
- But even then it was pretty successful.

00:07:21.381 --> 00:07:24.150
And so it had sold about 7 million albums.

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:26.820
The second, after the
release of those four singles

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:30.150
I just mentioned, sold
about two and a half.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.240
And it was like, okay-
- Which is still great.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:33.073
- It wasn't bad.

00:07:33.073 --> 00:07:34.860
It wasn't quite the
success of the first one.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.630
But, you know, we were licking our wounds

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.210
and back in the studio
making a third album.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:42.840
And then I got a call from
someone called Joel Schumacher.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:44.503
- Just someone, yeah.

00:07:44.503 --> 00:07:47.730
- God rest his soul, you know.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:51.183
And he said, "Look, Seal, I'm a huge fan.

00:07:52.868 --> 00:07:55.500
I'm making this movie
called "Batman Forever"

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.240
and I need a song for the love scene

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:01.267
between Kidman and
Kilmer," I think it was.

00:08:01.267 --> 00:08:02.280
"Have you got anything?"

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:03.930
My manager, Cavallo, Bob Cavallo,

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:05.497
was on the phone at the time.

00:08:05.497 --> 00:08:06.570
He said, "Have you got anything?"

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:08.310
I said, "Well, Joel, no I don't.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:11.610
I've finished promoting the second album.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:15.660
I released the singles. I'm sorry."

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:19.350
So Cavallo says, "Let
me take care of this."

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:23.460
So he sends Joel Schumacher
a copy of my second album

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:27.420
and puts a little asterisk
next to "Kiss from a Rose."

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:29.527
The next day Joel calls back, he says,

00:08:29.527 --> 00:08:32.130
"I temped the song in the scene.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:34.620
It doesn't work. Okay.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:37.530
But I love the song so much,
I'm just gonna stick it

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:38.967
on the end credits."

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:44.160
So, you know, 8 million
albums and four Grammys later,

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:45.930
everyone's like, well, you know-

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:48.180
- Oh my God! But you know what?

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.240
I love this story
because a lot of artists,

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:52.740
like, we always get told,

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:53.880
like I always tell artists too,

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:54.713
that I work with on "The Voice,"

00:08:54.713 --> 00:08:56.700
it's like, don't listen to all the chatter

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:59.220
all the time, just do what you're doing.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:01.350
It's gonna hit or it's not,
or you never know it's gonna

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.770
happen because it's just
timing sometimes, you know?

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:07.230
- So I owe so much to, you
know, talk about champions

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:08.763
and people seeing you.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:12.840
It could be argue that I owe my career

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.210
to Joel Schumacher, the
late Joel Schumacher.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.340
- And you're manager for
that little asterisk.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.800
Being like, "Thank you,
you should listen to this."

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:21.063
You're also touring.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:22.800
Tell us about the new tour.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:24.720
Like what's new, what can people expect?

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.970
- Okay, well it's a 30 year anniversary

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:28.740
of the first two albums.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:32.403
So the song that I'll sing tonight is,

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.550
that was some 30 years ago.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.090
And so what we've decided to do is to go

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.220
out and sing those two albums back

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:45.180
to back in their kind of studio form.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:48.957
And when I say studio, I
mean that the person who

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.820
is as much a part of my,

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:56.373
what people perceive to be my sound,

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:02.340
my producer Trevor Horn,
he's actually MDing the tour.

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:03.450
And he's never done that before.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:07.590
So he'll be the musical
director on the tour.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:08.940
So you'll hear those songs,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.836
anyone who has those early albums

00:10:11.836 --> 00:10:14.910
and a bunch of memories attached to them-

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:15.743
- Nostalgia for sure.

00:10:15.743 --> 00:10:17.680
- They'll be able to kind of relive

00:10:18.952 --> 00:10:22.260
that experience again with Trevor Horn.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:23.340
- [Kelly] Collaborative process though,

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:25.170
you came back to that.
- Exactly.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:26.640
- All right, we have to do another break

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:28.590
but you can see Seal on tour this spring.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:29.910
So go online for dates y'all.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:32.790
And look for the re-release
and deluxe edition of "Seal."

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:33.753
It's out now.

